<DocsLayout
  title="Plugins"
  subtitle="Extension manifests, worker-host APIs, assistant tools, and the separate browser plugin installation contract."
  description="CADCAMFUN plugin development: SDK manifests, capabilities, worker lifecycle, assistant tool loading, limits, and HTTP installation."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Choose the extension surface</h2>
<p><code>@cadcamfun/plugin-sdk</code> defines the worker plugin contract with a <code>plugin.json</code> manifest and API version <code>1</code>. Its host exposes capability-controlled services and runs plugin functions in Bun workers. The production assistant loader currently accepts plugins that request only <code>ai:tool</code>.</p>
    <p>The <code>/api/plugins</code> HTTP family uses a different browser-plugin contract: ZIP packages with <code>manifest.json</code>, a <code>main</code> entry, browser permissions, configuration, and JavaScript bootstrap. Installing a package through that API does not make it a worker SDK plugin. Use the manifest and lifecycle matching the intended integration.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="sdk" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Worker SDK manifest</h2>
<DocCode language="json" title="plugin.json for an assistant extension">
{`{
  "id": "com.example.shop-helper",
  "name": "Shop helper",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "apiVersion": "1",
  "entry": "index.js",
  "capabilities": ["ai:tool"],
  "contributes": {
    "aiTools": [{
      "id": "lookup",
      "description": "Look up the shop's documented inspection checklist.",
      "kind": "read",
      "parameters": { "type": "object", "properties": {} },
      "entry": "lookup"
    }]
  }
}`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Identifiers use a reverse-DNS form, the version is semantic <code>major.minor.patch</code> with an optional prerelease, and <code>apiVersion</code> must be <code>"1"</code>. Entry paths are relative to the plugin directory. Contributions can declare commands, panels, post-processors, operations, and assistant tools; declaring a contribution does not wire that feature into every host.</p>
    <p>The schema allows optional <code>signature</code> and <code>dev</code> fields. The current worker host records these values but does not perform cryptographic signature verification during install. A signature field alone is not evidence of a verified publisher.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="define-plugin" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Module entry and AI functions</h2>
<p><code>definePlugin</code> is a typed identity helper for a module with a manifest and <code>activate(ctx)</code>. Activation may return a cleanup function, and the module may expose <code>deactivate</code>. The SDK describes these lifecycle hooks; their availability depends on the concrete worker bootstrap, as detailed below.</p>
    <p>Assistant functions are exported through an <code>aiTools</code> record; the contribution's <code>entry</code> names the function in that record. A tool contribution includes its description, JSON parameter schema, and <code>read</code> or <code>write</code> kind. A write tool is routed through a human approval request in the embedded assistant; an outside bridge receives only permitted read plugin tools.</p>
    <p>Bundle the worker entry as a self-contained module. The worker host reads the entry source and imports it through a blob module. Treat relative package or file imports as a separate integration concern rather than assuming a Node-style filesystem loader.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="capabilities" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Capability-controlled APIs</h2>
<p>The SDK recognizes <code>drawing:read</code>, <code>drawing:write</code>, <code>toolpath:read</code>, <code>storage</code>, <code>ui:panel</code>, and <code>ai:tool</code>. Drawing write implies drawing read. The general host derives effective capabilities from the installed manifest and checks them on RPC; the embedding application decides which manifests and surfaces it is willing to load.</p>
    <p>The SDK context can expose drawing reads and Add/Update/RemoveEntity patches, toolpath/program reads, private string key-value storage, and declarative command/panel registration. Missing services are absent from the context. Storage keys use <code>plugin:&lt;id&gt;:</code> namespaces and reject absolute or parent-traversal keys.</p>
    <p>The production assistant loader supports only <code>ai:tool</code>. A manifest asking for drawing, toolpath, storage, or UI capabilities is skipped with a warning. It does not silently grant those surfaces or load their broader capabilities into the assistant host.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="host" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Loading and worker lifecycle</h2>
<p>To enable an assistant extension, place its <code>plugin.json</code> and bundled entry under <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/plugins/&lt;id&gt;/</code> and list the identifier in <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/plugins/enabled.json</code>, a JSON array. The folder identifier must match the manifest. Loading occurs while the assistant plugin service is built; a failed plugin logs a warning without stopping the application.</p>
    <p>The general host supports installed, enabled, disabled, and faulted states, with install, enable, disable, uninstall, command call, AI-tool call, and configuration operations. Enable creates a worker and completes its bootstrap handshake; disable tears it down. Uninstall removes the registry state and storage namespace through the host's injected dependencies.</p>
    <p>Workers start with an empty environment and the bootstrap removes selected global APIs. This is an application-level restriction, not an operating-system sandbox: Bun globals remain reachable. Enable only extension code that is appropriate to run inside the application process boundary.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="host-limits" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Runtime limits</h2>
<p>The host defines a 256 MB worker memory setting, a 2,000-call-per-minute rate limit, and at most 64 pending calls per plugin. Command timeout behavior is supplied by the embedding host; the assistant loader uses 10 seconds. AI tool calls have a separate 30-second deadline. Timeouts, absent tools, worker crashes, capability failures, and queue exhaustion return typed host failures or readable tool results.</p>
    <p>Crash tracking faults a plugin after three crashes within ten minutes. These host controls bound cooperative RPC and lifecycle behavior; the memory setting passed to Bun is not a substitute for a separately isolated executable.</p>
    <p>The limits are defined in <code>apps/sidecar/src/plugins/host.ts</code>: <code>CALL_TIMEOUT_MS</code> (5,000 ms per ordinary call), <code>AI_TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS</code> (30,000 ms per assistant tool call), <code>MEMORY_LIMIT_MB</code> (256), <code>CALL_RATE_LIMIT</code> (2,000 calls per plugin per minute), <code>MAX_PENDING_CALLS</code> (64), and <code>CRASH_LIMIT</code> (3 crashes within a 10-minute <code>CRASH_WINDOW_MS</code>, after which the plugin is disabled automatically).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="http" class="doc-section">
    <h2>ZIP installation and browser plugin API</h2>
<p><code>POST /api/plugins/install</code> accepts a multipart archive. The installer reads <code>manifest.json</code> from the ZIP root or a single top-level directory, validates the browser-plugin manifest, and stores its files under <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/storage/plugins/&lt;id&gt;/</code>. Browser manifests use <code>main</code>, <code>engines.cadcam</code>, and a browser permission list, rather than SDK <code>entry</code>, <code>apiVersion</code>, and capabilities.</p>
    <p><code>GET /api/plugins</code> lists those installed entries; detail, enable/disable, uninstall, update, and configuration endpoints operate on their registry. <code>/api/plugins/bootstrap.js?id=…</code> serves JavaScript; <code>/api/plugins/serve?id=…&amp;file=…</code> serves plugin files. These are text/binary operations with their declared content types.</p>
    <p>The local registry browser reads package metadata under <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/plugins-registry</code>. It is a local manifest catalogue. Archive installation, registry listing, and worker assistant extension loading are separate operations.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="capability-symbols" class="doc-section">
    <h2>SDK helpers</h2>
<p><code>effectiveCapabilities</code>, <code>hasCapability</code>, and <code>missingCapabilities</code> support capability inspection. <code>requireCapability</code> raises a capability error when a requested context surface is absent. <code>storageNamespace</code> and <code>storageKey</code> build namespaced keys; <code>parseManifest</code> validates JSON; <code>manifestKey</code> formats an ID/version pair.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="manifest-fields" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Implementation entry points</h2>
<p><code>packages/plugin-sdk/src</code> owns the public manifest, capability, context, plugin, and test-host APIs. <code>apps/sidecar/src/plugins/host.ts</code> owns worker lifecycle and RPC; <code>services/ai-plugins.ts</code> owns consent-file loading and assistant registration. Browser installation is implemented in <code>http/plugins.ts</code> and <code>repos/plugin-registry-repo.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Extension review</h2>
<p>Keep contribution schemas accurate, return small structured results, and declare read/write behavior honestly. Test against the actual embedding host: SDK availability does not establish production access to drawing, storage, UI, or post registries. The <a href="/docs/ai#nikcli-plugin">standalone nikcli plugin</a> is an HTTP action client and has a different purpose from code loaded into a worker.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="assistant-module" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Complete the sample assistant module</h2>
<p>The <code>plugin.json</code> example above names <code>aiTools.lookup</code>. This is the corresponding self-contained <code>index.js</code>. Top-level module code runs when imported; the declared lookup function runs when called.</p>
    <DocCode language="js" title="index.js">
{String.raw`export const aiTools = {
  lookup: async () => ({
    ok: true,
    checklist: [
      "Compare the drawing revision with the job traveller.",
      "Record the measuring instrument and its calibration status.",
      "Record each inspected dimension and the measured value."
    ]
  })
}`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>These are sample shop instructions, not a built-in inspection standard. Replace them with the content your extension actually owns. To admit this module at the next service initialization, the enabled file is:</p>
    <DocCode language="json" title="&lt;dataDir&gt;/plugins/enabled.json">
{String.raw`["com.example.shop-helper"]`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The running catalogue exposes an identifier derived from plugin and tool IDs. Discover it through <code>GET /api/ai/actions</code>; do not assume the browser ZIP registry is the assistant's enabled list. Reloading the application/service is the reliable way to reread the production consent file; no filesystem watcher is configured by this loader.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>apps/sidecar/src/services/ai-plugins.ts</code>, <code>packages/ai/src/tools/plugin-tool.ts</code>, and <code>apps/sidecar/test/ai-plugin-tools.test.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="schema-and-lifecycle" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Argument schemas and lifecycle limits</h2>
<p>A tool's local ID must start with a lowercase letter and contain lowercase letters, digits, or underscores, with a 40-character maximum. Descriptions contain 10–2,000 characters. The contribution must name a non-empty entry function and a JSON parameter schema. The application adapter validates arguments before calling the worker; a refusal can return a structured <code>ok: false</code> result without terminating the plugin.</p>
    <p>The public SDK describes <code>activate</code>, cleanup, and <code>deactivate</code> entry points. The current Bun worker bootstrap's activate/deactivate RPC handlers acknowledge lifecycle messages but do not invoke those exported module functions. Assistant extensions should therefore put their callable behavior in <code>aiTools</code> and avoid depending on SDK activation callbacks for setup or cleanup.</p>
    <p>The general worker bootstrap also contains placeholder drawing/toolpath/UI handlers. They do not constitute production drawing or UI integration. The assistant loader's restriction to <code>ai:tool</code> prevents these unsupported surfaces from being advertised as working application access.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>packages/plugin-sdk/src/manifest.ts</code>, <code>plugin.ts</code>, <code>apps/sidecar/src/plugins/host.ts</code> (<code>PLUGIN_BOOTSTRAP</code>), and <code>services/ai-plugins.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Diagnose a missing or failing extension</h2>
<ul>
      <li><strong>No tools in the catalogue:</strong> check valid enabled JSON, exact folder/manifest ID match, manifest decoding, and the <code>ai:tool</code> capability. Read the startup warning for a skipped plugin.</li>
      <li><strong>Manifest requests unsupported surfaces:</strong> remove unsupported capabilities only if the code truly does not need them. An assistant plugin cannot acquire drawing-write access merely by declaring it.</li>
      <li><strong>NoSuchTool:</strong> make <code>contributes.aiTools[].entry</code> match an exported function under <code>aiTools</code>. The contribution ID and entry name serve different purposes.</li>
      <li><strong>Argument validation failure:</strong> inspect the JSON schema and required fields. Wrong values are refused before execution, as demonstrated by the real-worker and application integration tests.</li>
      <li><strong>Timeout or faulted state:</strong> investigate blocking work, unresolved promises, or crashes; repeated crashes fault the plugin. Re-enable only after correcting the cause.</li>
      <li><strong>ZIP installer rejects the manifest:</strong> use the browser-plugin <code>manifest.json</code> contract for that endpoint. Renaming a worker <code>plugin.json</code> does not translate its fields.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Source: <code>apps/sidecar/test/plugins-ai-tools.test.ts</code>, <code>ai-plugin-tools.test.ts</code>, <code>plugins-http.test.ts</code>, and <code>src/plugins/host.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

